VERDICT: FAIL -- the AT-SPSX (1000BASE-SX) module accepts `speed 100` and `speed auto 100`.
         The port then reads "connected" at 100 Mb/s, and 0 of 100 frames cross the fibre.
  Method (the database has no steps): the DUT's two SX modules, port1.0.25 and port1.0.26,
  are joined by one fibre. Each port is in a different VLAN, so frames from tb470 eth2 must
  cross the fibre to reach tb470 eth1. Each row sets a speed or duplex on port1.0.25. Then
  eth2 sends 100 TEST frames to eth1 and 100 CONTROL frames that the DUT must drop.
  Expected: a 1000BASE-SX module runs only 1000 Mb/s full duplex. The DUT accepts the
  settings that give 1000/full and passes 100 of 100 frames with them. The DUT refuses every
  other speed and duplex.
      auto-negotiation ............... connected a-full a-1000, 100/100 frames  (STEP 1)
      speed 1000 ..................... connected 1000, 100/100 frames  (STEP 2)
      speed 1000 + duplex full ....... both ends set: connected, 100/100 frames  (STEP 2)
      speed 100, speed auto 100 ...... ACCEPTED, reads connected 100, 0/100 frames  (STEP 3)
      speed 10, 2500, 10000 .......... refused, link stays at 1000, 100/100 frames  (STEP 4)
      duplex half .................... refused, link stays at 1000, 100/100 frames  (STEP 4)
      control frames ................. 0/100 at eth1 in every row
FAIL CONDITION: STEP 3. Expected: `speed 100` and `speed auto 100` are refused, because
         1000BASE-SX has no 100 Mb/s mode. Seen: the DUT accepted both with no error line.
         The two ends of one fibre then read different speeds: 100 on port1.0.25 and 1000 on
         port1.0.26. The DUT counted 101 frames out of port1.0.25, and port1.0.26 counted 0
         in. 0 of 100 frames reached eth1. The same path passed 100 of 100 before and after
         this setting, so the method is not the cause. The DUT accepts a setting the module
         cannot run, and it reports a link that carries no traffic.
TRIED:   `speed 100` (0/100 frames) and `speed auto 100` (0/100 frames).
CAVEAT:  One module type (AT-SPSX), one pair of modules, looped on one DUT. The DUT is
         also its own link partner, so a different partner was not tested.
